# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-eval-request-form-reenters-its-own-temp-file — MISDIAGNOSED: the cause was the loader dropping the second form on a line
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: alfe-backend
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-09-26

* Observed

Found while fixing alfe-autocad-error-primitive-masks-load-failure, with
the clautolisp-hosted protocol harness (no CAD needed). A file is loaded
through a =(load "file")= REQUEST, and the file contains a form that
fails:

#+begin_example
single.lsp:   ;; single
              (setq single-ran 1)(/ 1 0)

request 1:  (load ".../single.lsp")   ->  DONE 1 OK      (no diagnostic)
request 2:  (princ 1)                 ->  DONE 2 OK      prints 1
#+end_example

The division by zero is never reported. Worse, it is not always lost in
the same way -- in another ordering the SAME failure surfaced against
the FOLLOWING request:

#+begin_example
request 1:  (load ".../outer.lsp")    ->  DONE 1 OK
request 2:  (princ …)                 ->  DONE 2 FAIL
            ERROR protocol request 2: Division by zero in /.
#+end_example

so a later, innocent request is blamed for an earlier failure.

By contrast, a failure the SOURCE LOADER raises itself is reported
correctly, with the full source-aware diagnostic:

#+begin_example
outer.lsp:   (load "/nonexistent-file-xyz")
request 1 -> DONE 1 FAIL
  ERROR protocol request 1: while loading …/outer.lsp (form starting at
  line 1) at line 1, column 1: LOAD failed: "/nonexistent-file-xyz"
#+end_example

That is the discriminator: the loss happens only for a form that goes
through alfe's own =autolisp-eval-request-form= override.

* Cause

The override (emitted into run-common.lsp by
=autolisp-front-end/source/file-protocol.lisp=) evaluates a non-LOAD
form by writing it to a temp file and calling the host's native LOAD:

#+begin_src lisp
(setq path (strcat *AUTOLISP_PROTOCOL_DIR* "alfe-eval.lsp"))
(setq text (autolisp-readable-text form))
… write "(setq *AUTOLISP-EVAL-RESULT* <text>)" to path …
(setq err (vl-catch-all-apply 'load (list path)))
#+end_src

The path is FIXED, one per session. And the override is not called only
once per request: =autolisp-source-load-run-body-impl= (in the
bootstrap) calls =autolisp-eval-request-form= for EVERY FORM of a loaded
file. So while the outer =load= of alfe-eval.lsp -- or of the user's
file -- is still being read, a nested invocation rewrites the very file
being read. The override re-enters itself through a shared mutable file.

That is consistent with all three observations: a failure vanishing, a
failure appearing against the next request, and the outcome depending on
the order of the forms.

The =(load …)= FORM path and the =-l FILE= ACTION path differ here,
which is why this was not seen earlier: the action goes through
=autolisp-run-load=, which reports failures correctly (that is the run
in the EPURE API probe that reported DONE 1 FAIL, exit 1). It is the
FORM path -- what SCHME+'s =(tu:load …)= entry points use -- that loses
them.

* Why it matters

It is the same class of defect as
issues/closed/alfe-eval-x-output-lost-on-cad (a failure reported as a
success), which is the one that cost a whole investigation: a user
cannot tell a working run from a broken one, and a script that tests
alfe's exit code sees success. Here it is worse in one respect: the
blame can land on an unrelated later request.

It also blocks acceptance criterion 2 of
alfe-autocad-error-primitive-masks-load-failure (a nested loaded-file
failure must reach alfe with the original text): the ERROR dependency is
gone, but a failure on THIS path is still swallowed.

* Fix direction

The temp file must not be shared between nesting levels. Either

- give each invocation its own file (a depth counter or a per-request
  sequence in the name, cleaned up after), or
- do not re-enter at all: evaluate nested forms with plain =eval= and
  keep the file trick for the request's OUTERMOST form only, which is
  where it was needed (the BricsCAD V26 mapcar/lambda mis-dispatch that
  motivated it -- see issues/closed/bricscad-rest-mapcar-lambda-bug).

Then check the outcome is reported: the request that contained the
failing form must be the one that FAILS, with the engine's own message.

* Reproduction (no CAD)

=drive-hosted-engine= in autolisp-front-end/tests/file-protocol-tests.lisp
hosts the emitted runtime under clautolisp. Stage a file containing
=(setq x 1)(/ 1 0)=, send =(load "<that file>")= as one request and
=(princ 1)= as the next, and read the statuses: the first is OK.

* Acceptance criteria

- A form that fails inside a loaded file makes THAT request fail, with
  the engine's message and the file/line the source loader assembles.
- No failure is ever attributed to a later request.
- The BricsCAD mapcar/lambda behaviour that motivated the file trick is
  preserved (its regression test stays green).
- A regression test in the hosted harness covers both the single-level
  and the nested case.

* Resolved — MISDIAGNOSED. The cause was elsewhere; clautolisp 2.2.100

The symptoms above are real and are fixed, but not by anything in this
issue's /Cause/ section. The re-entrancy it describes does not happen:
the override is the one thing the source loader calls per form, and when
that form is a =(load …)= the override routes it to
=autolisp-eval-load-form=, which uses NO temp file. So the fixed
filename is never re-entered, and giving it a per-depth name changed
nothing (measured: the same DONE OK).

What the evidence actually showed. After a failing load, only
=alfe-eval-1.lsp= existed in the protocol directory, and it held the
FIRST form of the loaded file, =(setq single-ran 1)= — not the division
that was supposed to fail. The failing form had never been evaluated.
Because both forms sat on ONE LINE, and the read loop called READ on the
whole balanced buffer, took the first form, and then cleared the buffer.
The second form of every line was silently discarded.

Proof, through the CAD-side loader, before the fix:

#+begin_example
twoforms.lsp:  (princ "x")(princ "y")
               (setq a 1)(setq b 2)

stdout: "x(A 1 B UNBOUND)"        ;; y never printed, b never set
status: DONE OK, stderr empty
#+end_example

clautolisp's OWN loader runs both (=(A 1 B 2)|xy=), so this was alfe's
CAD-side loader alone.

Filed and fixed as
=issues/closed/alfe-cad-source-loader-drops-a-second-form-on-a-line.issue=.
With that fixed the failing form runs and its failure is reported:
=DONE 1 FAIL= with /Division by zero in ./, at one nesting level and at
two, and the next request stays OK — which is exactly this issue's
acceptance list, so it is met, by the other fix.

Kept as a record rather than deleted: the misdiagnosis is instructive.
The "failure attributed to the NEXT request" observation that made
re-entrancy plausible has a simpler explanation — the pending error from
the discarded-form ordering surfaced on a later turn — and the lesson is
that a shared-file hypothesis should have been checked by LOOKING at the
files (which is what finally settled it) before being written up as the
cause.
